# Humble Bundle website migration investigation

Investigation date: November 11, 2026  
Website behavior is undocumented and may change after this date.

## Executive summary

Humble Bundle removed the private REST endpoints used by this repository and by `../dl`. The redesigned account site is a Next.js application backed by authenticated server actions and a new entitlement-oriented data model.

There is no discovered stable, documented replacement API. The closest functional replacement is a Next.js server action named `filterUserLibrary`. It should be treated as an internal implementation detail: its generated identifier and request protocol can change with any frontend deployment.

The most practical migration is a browser-assisted collector that logs in through the new authentication system, reads the complete library, and normalizes the returned entitlements into the legacy order structure consumed by this downloader.

## Verified endpoint behavior

Requests made with the locally configured, unexpired legacy `_simpleauth_sess` value produced these results:

| URL | Result on November 11, 2026 |
| --- | --- |
| `https://www.humblebundle.com/api/v1/user/order` | HTTP 404 with the new site's HTML response |
| `https://hr-humblebundle.appspot.com/api/v1/user/order` | HTTP 403 |
| `https://www.humblebundle.com/home/library` | Next.js account application; unauthenticated content initiates login behavior |
| `https://www.humblebundle.com/home/purchases` | Redirect to `auth.humblebundle.com` login |

The configured legacy session had an expiration date of November 13, 2026, so these results were not caused simply by its recorded expiration.

## New website architecture

The current `/home/library` page is served by Next.js and advertises routes including:

- `/home/library`
- `/home/library/:id`
- `/home/library/content/:id`
- `/home/library/group/:id`
- `/home/purchases`
- `/home/purchases/:id`

The frontend contains generated references to these server actions:

- `filterUserLibrary`
- `getVaultSignedUrls`
- `revalidateLibraryAction`

The observed build-specific action identifier for `filterUserLibrary` was:

```text
60d7ee9f87dea9e5c62af33064bf64365206a33aaf
```

This identifier is evidence of the action's existence, not a supported API contract. It is expected to change when Humble deploys another frontend build.

The website also exposes `GET /api/auth/session-status` to its own frontend, but that is a session-state check rather than a library-data endpoint.

## New data model

The frontend represents purchased content approximately as:

```text
library item
  id
  title
  itemType
  type
  deliveryMethods[]
  entitlements
    keys[]
    downloads[]
      id
      downloads[]
        key
        downloadStruct[]
          name
          size
          url.web
```

It also supports `entitlement-group` records and distinguishes bundle, storefront, subscription content, and vault content. Vault URLs may be signed on demand through `getVaultSignedUrls`.

The legacy saved data uses:

```text
order
  product
  gamekey
  subproducts[]
    machine_name
    human_name
    downloads[]
      machine_name
      platform
      download_identifier
      download_version_number
      download_struct[]
        name
        file_size
        md5
        url.web
```

The structures are similar enough to normalize downloadable entitlements for the existing downloader. Some legacy metadata, such as order-level spending and possibly MD5 hashes, may not be present in the new model.

## Authentication implications

The website now sends account login through `auth.humblebundle.com`, using a flow URL and returning to the main site afterward. Supplying only the old `_simpleauth_sess` cookie did not authenticate `/home/purchases`, despite the locally recorded session not yet being expired.

Consequently, updating only the endpoint strings will not repair this repository. A collector must participate in the new login flow or reuse the full set of cookies produced by a logged-in browser session.

Credentials and cookie values were not written to this repository or to these notes.

